{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 3=6.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(8-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(1+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{10M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{10}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,4\\le y\\le 6}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[y]_{4}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{7}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(7+9-\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{40}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{20}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{4}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(4+9-(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(13-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{34}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{17}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{19}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $x={y}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{70}}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2},$ the line $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{20M}{7}(x+y).$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{20M}{7}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{14}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{14}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{7}\\cdot \\frac{5}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{100M}{147}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100M}{147}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100}{147}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{7\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{7}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{7\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the line $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=\\sqrt{x}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12M(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =12M\\cdot \\frac{7}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{21M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=20Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 20Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{7}-{x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}-\\frac{1}{11}{x}^{11}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{11}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{88} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5M}{22}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=x,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{4}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region between two circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2$ for $x\\ge 0.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{y}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}{sin}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +1}{8}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{8}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{3\\theta }{8}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }{32}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{M\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies region bounded by two circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{14\\pi }M\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{14\\pi }Mr.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{93M}{70}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{93}{70}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{15}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{52}{41}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{67}{60}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{93}{70}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{93}{70}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ in the first quadrant. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{8}Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{8}Mr\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-38825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97453585e2e0358412264d6f49a6f0b2b00e21a7c7613becd4e93bde35d829ea"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "q-163594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/state.json" "file_sha256" "3be43f83f2a07a6d2d0e9df8683c4ad6db33f39a4a309b973ca08a65fe0dd50c"} {"question" "e-12487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12487.json" "file_sha256" "23c064685ccfc1c6eb5c5df9f9fd997a9adaddc472a4385dafdfd21d53f09a69"} {"question" "e-12487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} =  3\\cdot 1 = 3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3M(2-1) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} =  3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12487.json" "file_sha256" "23c064685ccfc1c6eb5c5df9f9fd997a9adaddc472a4385dafdfd21d53f09a69"} {"question" "e-12507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y = x,$ $x = 1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 3Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12507.json" "file_sha256" "4fa4c80bba1e6b99094e4e565fc6ab74e2ba6fff70bae4075cad5b3523d24401"} {"question" "e-12507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{[\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M\\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12507.json" "file_sha256" "4fa4c80bba1e6b99094e4e565fc6ab74e2ba6fff70bae4075cad5b3523d24401"} {"question" "e-12495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A plate of mass $M$ occupies one-half of the circle $x^2+y^2\\leq 1$ for $y\\geq 0.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda(x,y)=\\dfrac{3}{2}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12495.json" "file_sha256" "b93885830e036eb492c2d1c9933cf5f913a71750360346cdbd6e11d377ca2b8f"} {"question" "e-12495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda(r,\\theta)=\\dfrac{3}{2}Mr\\sin\\theta.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M({\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073981/example-12495.json" "file_sha256" "b93885830e036eb492c2d1c9933cf5f913a71750360346cdbd6e11d377ca2b8f"}] "usage" {"q-38825" [] "q-163011" [] "q-163604" [] "e-12507" [] "e-12495" [] "e-12487" [] "q-38824" [] "q-163612" []} "basis" 2714 "retractions" [[17592186198592 :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 4 \\le y \\le 6}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis."] [17592186198578 :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 1 \\le y \\le 3}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin."] [17592186198883 :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $x = y^{2}$. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = 12Mxy$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis."] [17592186198591 :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y = x$, $x = 2$, and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{4}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis."] [17592186198884 :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 4$ in the first quadrant. Its mass density at any point is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{8}Mx$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis."] [17592186197987 :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis."] [17592186197987 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 3\\cdot 1 = 3\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)x^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{0}^{3}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}9dy \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{1}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3M(2 - 1) \\\\\n&= 3M\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} = 3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}\n$$"] [17592186197991 :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y = x$, $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 3Mx$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin."] [17592186197991 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{0} &= ∬_(D)(x^{2} + y^{2})λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) \\cdot 3Mxdydx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}x^{3} + xy^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}[x^{3}y + \\frac{1}{3}xy^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M[\\frac{4}{15}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 3M \\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{0} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{I_{0}}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186197989 :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies one-half of the circle $x^2+y^2\\leq 1$ for $y\\geq 0$. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda(x,y)=\\dfrac{3}{2}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin."] [17592186197989 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta)=\\dfrac{3}{2}Mr\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{0} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ)r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M(∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}dr)(∫_{0}^{π}\\sin θdθ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}r^{5} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\cdot (-\\cos θ) \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{0} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{I_{0}}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{3M}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194370 17592186194371 17592186194372 17592186194373 17592186194374 17592186194448 17592186194449 17592186194450 17592186194451 17592186194452 17592186194454 17592186194455 17592186194456 17592186194457 17592186194458 17592186197534 17592186197535 17592186197536 17592186197537 17592186197538 17592186197540 17592186197541 17592186197542 17592186197543 17592186197544] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194369 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194369 :answer-field/id] [17592186194369 :answer-field/key] [17592186194369 :answer-field/type] [17592186194447 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194447 :answer-field/id] [17592186194447 :answer-field/key] [17592186194447 :answer-field/type] [17592186194453 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194453 :answer-f